=== MDV-P0-004
title: GitHub repository protections and PR pipeline (build, analyzers, tests, CodeQL, dependency and secret scanning)
type: Task
epic: MDV-P0-E01
prd: P0-01
covers: exit:4
priority: MUST
roles: Developer
labels: ci, security
estimate: 1.5
weeks: 1
depends: MDV-P0-001
confidence: 90 | Standard GitHub Actions setup; requirement is explicit.
sources: PRD v2 P0-01; docs/03-architecture/repository-structure.md#repository-rules-build-plan-b-04-adapted-to-prd-v2
desc:
Create .github/workflows/pr.yml running on every pull request: dotnet build with analyzers as errors, dotnet test (unit + Testcontainers integration), pnpm lint, tsc --noEmit, vitest, CodeQL (C# + TypeScript), dependency review, secret scanning with push protection, Dependabot for NuGet/npm/GitHub Actions. Protect main: PR required, status checks required, linear history, no force push, signed tags for releases. Add CODEOWNERS and a PR template with the X1–X8 checklist.
ac:
- A PR that fails build, tests, lint, type-check, CodeQL or secret scanning cannot be merged into main.
- Direct pushes and force pushes to main are rejected.
- Dependabot opens update PRs for NuGet, npm and Actions weekly.
- The PR template contains the X1–X8 Definition-of-Done checklist and a "synthetic data only" confirmation.
- Pipeline duration for the skeleton is under 10 minutes.

=== MDV-P0-005
title: Local development environment with Docker Compose (SQL Server, Azurite, mail and SMS sinks)
type: Task
epic: MDV-P0-E01
prd: P0-01
priority: MUST
roles: Developer
labels: dev-env
estimate: 1
weeks: 1
depends: MDV-P0-001
confidence: 90 | Explicit in P0-01; well-known tooling.
sources: PRD v2 P0-01, §08 Environments
desc:
docker-compose.yml with SQL Server 2022 (Developer), Azurite (Blob), smtp4dev (email sink) and a fake SMS endpoint, plus a one-command start (`make dev` or `pnpm dev`) that applies EF Core migrations and seeds the synthetic tenant. Local identity uses a development Entra External ID tenant or a dev-only OIDC stub that is impossible to enable outside Development.
ac:
- Given Docker is running, when I run the documented start command, then API, web/clinic and all dependencies start and the seeded synthetic tenant can sign in within 5 minutes on a clean machine.
- The dev OIDC stub refuses to start unless ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Development (verified by a test).
- No real secrets are needed locally; .env.example lists variable names only.
- Emails and SMS sent locally appear in the sinks and are never delivered externally.

=== MDV-P0-006
title: Azure environments as code (Bicep): App Service, Azure SQL private endpoint, Blob, Key Vault, App Insights, Defender, budgets
type: Task
epic: MDV-P0-E01
prd: P0-02
priority: MUST
roles: Developer
labels: infra, bicep, azure
estimate: 3
weeks: 1-2
depends: MDV-P0-003
confidence: 80 | Resources are explicit; private networking cost and region SKU availability still need confirming in the subscription.
sources: PRD v2 P0-02, §07b; docs/03-architecture/azure-deployment.md
desc:
infra/bicep/main.bicep + modules (network, appservice, sql, storage, keyvault, monitoring, defender, budgets) and parameter files for dev, staging and prod in the launch country (UAE parameter files created but not deployed unless ADR-002 says UAE). App Service (Linux, .NET 10) with VNet integration and a staging slot; Azure SQL with Entra-only auth, private endpoint, public network access disabled; Storage with private endpoint and no public blob access; Key Vault with RBAC, soft delete and purge protection; Application Insights + Log Analytics; Defender for Cloud plans; budgets and cost alerts per environment; Azure Policy denying resources outside the allowed region(s).
ac:
- `az deployment sub what-if` and a real deployment of dev succeed from CI with no manual portal steps.
- Azure SQL `publicNetworkAccess` is Disabled and only reachable through the private endpoint (verified by a connectivity test from outside the VNet failing).
- Key Vault has purge protection enabled; Storage has `allowBlobPublicAccess=false`.
- A budget with email alerts at 50/80/100 % exists for each environment.
- Azure Policy assignment denies creating resources in any region other than the configured country region(s).
- No secret value appears in any Bicep or parameter file.
notes:
- Private endpoints add cost; capture the monthly estimate in the PR description (Software Architecture §9.3).

=== MDV-P0-007
title: GitHub OIDC federation to Azure and tagged deploy to staging
type: Task
epic: MDV-P0-E01
prd: P0-01, P0-02
covers: exit:4, pkg:1
priority: MUST
roles: Developer
labels: cd, azure
estimate: 1.5
weeks: 2
depends: MDV-P0-004, MDV-P0-006
confidence: 88 | Standard pattern; only App Service slot swap specifics to tune.
sources: PRD v2 P0-01, P0-02, §08
desc:
Federated credentials (one app identity per environment) so GitHub Actions deploys without stored cloud secrets. deploy-staging.yml runs on a signed tag `v0.*`: build once, run EF Core migration bundle with the migration identity, deploy to the staging slot, smoke test, swap. Production workflow exists but requires a manual approval and only accepts tags from main.
ac:
- No Azure client secret or publish profile exists in GitHub secrets (only federated credentials).
- Pushing tag v0.0.1 on main deploys to staging end-to-end and the health endpoint returns 200 with the tag version.
- A tag on a non-main commit is rejected by the workflow.
- Migrations run with the dedicated migration database user, not the app identity.
- Deploy is repeatable: re-running the same tag is idempotent.

=== MDV-P0-008
title: Database security baseline part 1: Entra-only auth, managed identity, least-privilege users per module and for migrations
type: Task
epic: MDV-P0-E03
prd: P0-09
priority: MUST
roles: Developer
labels: database, security
estimate: 1.5
weeks: 2
depends: MDV-P0-006
confidence: 85 | Azure SQL features are well documented; per-module schema permissions need care with EF Core.
sources: PRD v2 P0-09, §07b layers 01–02; docs/03-architecture/data-model.md
desc:
Configure Microsoft Entra-only authentication on the logical server. The App Service managed identity is the only application principal. Create one database user/role per module schema (identity, tenancy, audit, … ) with GRANT only on its own schema, a separate migration principal with DDL rights, and a read-only reporting role. Connection strings use `Authentication=Active Directory Managed Identity; Encrypt=Strict`. No SQL logins with passwords exist.
ac:
- Attempting SQL authentication with a username/password fails (Entra-only is on).
- The app identity cannot ALTER/CREATE objects; the migration identity can; verified by integration test against Testcontainers SQL Server using equivalent roles.
- A module's database role cannot SELECT from another module's schema (architecture/integration test).
- Connection strings in App Service settings contain no password; `Encrypt=Strict` is enforced.
- Human access to production data is only via just-in-time elevation (documented runbook, links MDV-P0-025).

=== MDV-P0-009
title: Database security baseline part 2: Always Encrypted (national/insurance IDs), dynamic data masking, TDE with customer-managed key
type: Task
epic: MDV-P0-E03
prd: P0-09
priority: MUST
roles: Developer
labels: database, encryption
estimate: 2.5
weeks: 2-3
depends: MDV-P0-008
confidence: 72 | Always Encrypted with EF Core and VBS enclaves has query limitations (PRD risk: MED); design of deterministic vs randomized columns needs a short spike.
sources: PRD v2 P0-09, §07b layers 03–07, risk "Always Encrypted limits queries"
desc:
Column master key in Key Vault usable only by the API identity; column encryption keys for: Aadhaar last-4, ABHA number/address, Emirates ID, passport, visa/residency number, insurance member number. Deterministic encryption for exact-match lookups (EID, ABHA), randomized + VBS enclave where range/LIKE is required. Dynamic data masking on phone, email and date of birth for support/reporting roles. TDE protector switched to a customer-managed key with automatic rotation.
ac:
- A DBA/admin querying an encrypted column (SSMS without CMK access) sees ciphertext only.
- The API can insert and look up a patient by exact Emirates ID and by exact ABHA through EF Core (integration test with a test CMK).
- A login in the support role sees masked phone/email/DOB values.
- TDE protector is a Key Vault key with rotation policy; backups inherit it.
- The protected-column list is documented in the data classification register (MDV-P0-016) and kept deliberately small.
tests:
- Integration test proves an exact-match lookup on deterministic EID works and a LIKE query is rejected without enclave.

=== MDV-P0-010
title: Tenancy model with EF Core global filters and Azure SQL row-level security (cross-tenant test in CI)
type: Story
epic: MDV-P0-E02
prd: P0-04
covers: exit:3
priority: MUST
roles: All staff
labels: tenancy, rls, security
estimate: 3
weeks: 2
depends: MDV-P0-001, MDV-P0-008
confidence: 88 | Pattern specified in PRD v2 P0-04 and ADR-005; main risk is connection-pool session-context handling, covered by the tests.
sources: PRD v2 P0-04, §07b layer 06; docs/adr/ADR-005-tenant-isolation-rls.md
desc:
Organisation (tenant) → facility → membership model. `tenant_id` (PRD: clinic_id) on every clinic-owned table. A DbConnection interceptor sets SESSION_CONTEXT(N'tenant_id') read-only on every open from the authenticated membership; a SQL security policy applies FILTER and BLOCK predicates; EF Core global query filters mirror it. One user can hold memberships in several clinics; the active facility is chosen at sign-in.
ac:
- Given two tenants with data, when user A of tenant 1 queries any tenant-owned table, then no row of tenant 2 is returned, even when the EF query filter is disabled with IgnoreQueryFilters() (RLS still blocks).
- Inserting or updating a row with another tenant's tenant_id is blocked by the BLOCK predicate.
- A user with memberships in two clinics sees only the active facility's tenant data and can switch facility, which re-establishes session context.
- Session context is reset on every pooled connection open (test opens 100 pooled connections alternating tenants with zero leaks).
- The cross-tenant read/write test runs in CI on every PR and fails the build on any leak (P0 exit gate 3).

=== MDV-P0-011
title: Staff sign-in through the BFF with Entra External ID: MFA (TOTP), passkeys and recovery
type: Story
epic: MDV-P0-E02
prd: P0-03
screens: F-03
priority: MUST
roles: All staff
labels: identity, auth
estimate: 3
weeks: 2
depends: MDV-P0-007
confidence: 78 | Flow and copy are designed (F-03); Entra External ID passkey and custom-UI capabilities must be validated in a short spike before final UI.
sources: design/Medvya Access and Onboarding (F-03); docs/02-design/screen-specs.md#f-03-sign-in-and-mfa-3-steps; docs/adr/ADR-004-bff-session.md
desc:
Implement OIDC Authorization Code + PKCE against Microsoft Entra External ID inside Host.Api (BFF). Tokens stay server-side; the browser gets an HTTP-only, Secure, SameSite=Strict session cookie and an antiforgery token. MFA is mandatory for all staff (TOTP authenticator; passkeys preferred; SMS OTP only as fallback where allowed). Account recovery flow. Screen F-03: email or mobile → "Continue with passkey" / "Use a one-time code instead" → 6-digit code → facility picker.
ac:
- A staff user cannot reach any authenticated page or API without completing MFA.
- After sign-in no access or refresh token is readable from JavaScript (document.cookie and storage contain none).
- Wrong code shows "Incorrect code. 2 attempts left before a 15-minute lock." and the account is locked for 15 minutes after the final failure (rate-limited server-side).
- Passkey-unsupported browsers fall back to the one-time code path with a clear message.
- An SSO-enforced tenant (placeholder flag for P7) hides password/OTP options and shows "Hospital staff? Sign in with SSO".
- Recovery ("Can't sign in?") re-verifies identity and forces MFA re-enrolment; it never reveals whether an account exists.
- Sign-in, failure, lockout and recovery events are written to the audit log (MDV-P0-017).

=== MDV-P0-012
title: Session control: facility picker, idle timeout on shared clinic PCs, device/session revocation
type: Story
epic: MDV-P0-E02
prd: P0-03
screens: F-03
priority: MUST
roles: All staff
labels: identity, session
estimate: 2
weeks: 2-3
depends: MDV-P0-011, MDV-P0-010
confidence: 85 | Behaviour specified (F-03 step 3, F-05a); idle-timeout values per tenant need a default.
sources: PRD v2 P0-03; design/Medvya Access and Onboarding (F-03, F-05a)
desc:
After MFA the user picks a facility ("Choose where you are working today") listing each membership with role. `/v1/me/context` returns user, active membership, permissions and feature flags. Configurable idle timeout per tenant (default 15 min on shared-device mode, 8 h max session); unsaved drafts are kept locally (encrypted, no PHI in plain localStorage) and on re-auth the user must re-confirm the patient (F-05a). Users can see and revoke their sessions/devices; admins can revoke any staff session.
ac:
- Given a user with two memberships, when they sign in, then they must choose a facility and the session is scoped to it; switching facility re-scopes without re-entering MFA.
- After the idle timeout the session ends and the "Session expired" screen (F-05a) appears; signing in again returns to the previous route and asks to confirm the patient before continuing.
- Revoking a session from "My devices" or by an admin ends it within 60 seconds on the next request.
- No patient data is stored in localStorage/sessionStorage in clear text (automated check in Playwright).
- `/v1/me/context` never returns permissions the role does not grant.

=== MDV-P0-013
title: Invite a colleague and accept the invitation (membership invited → active)
type: Story
epic: MDV-P0-E02
prd: P0-03
screens: F-06
priority: MUST
roles: Practice admin, Staff
labels: identity, invitations
estimate: 2
weeks: 3
depends: MDV-P0-011, MDV-P0-014
confidence: 88 | Hi-fi F-06 exists with copy, scope and expiry rules.
sources: design/Medvya Access and Onboarding (F-06); docs/02-design/screen-specs.md#f-06-invite-acceptance
desc:
An admin invites a person by email or mobile with a role and facility scope. The invitee opens /invite/:token, sees "Join {clinic} … invited you as {Role}", the capability list (✓ allowed / ✕ not allowed, e.g. reception cannot see clinical notes), facility scope and hours, then creates a passkey (or MFA) and joins. Membership moves invited → active. Expired or reused tokens show "Ask your admin to resend".
ac:
- Invitation tokens are single-use, random (≥128 bits), expire after the configured period (default 7 days) and are stored hashed.
- The acceptance screen shows role, facility scope and capability list exactly as derived from the role-permission matrix.
- After acceptance the membership is active and the user lands on the role's home route.
- An expired, revoked or already-used token shows the expired state and creates no membership.
- Invites and acceptances are audited; the invite email/SMS contains no patient data.
- Clinician invites are flagged "licence verification required" (verification itself ships in P1, MDV-P1-007).

=== MDV-P0-014
title: Server-side authorisation from the Foundations role-permission matrix
type: Story
epic: MDV-P0-E02
prd: P0-05
covers: pkg:2
priority: MUST
roles: All
labels: authorisation, security
estimate: 3
weeks: 2-3
depends: MDV-P0-010
confidence: 87 | Matrix is defined (docs/02-design/sitemap-and-permissions.md); relationship/purpose checks for clinical data become real in P2.
sources: PRD v2 P0-05; docs/02-design/sitemap-and-permissions.md#role-permission-matrix-foundations-06
desc:
Encode the role-permission matrix (solo doctor, doctor, receptionist, nurse, practice admin, care coordinator, patient; hospital roles later) as capabilities. ASP.NET Core authorization policies check tenant, facility, role/capability, and — as hooks for P2 — care relationship and purpose. Every endpoint must declare a policy; an architecture test fails the build for any endpoint without one. Export the matrix as docs (role matrix is part of the P0 delivery package).
ac:
- Every API endpoint has an explicit authorization policy; an automated test enumerates endpoints and fails if any is anonymous by accident (allow-list for public endpoints).
- A receptionist calling a clinical-note endpoint gets 403 regardless of UI (security test), and the attempt is audited as "Denied".
- Capabilities per role match the Foundations matrix cell-by-cell (table-driven test generated from docs/02-design/data/foundations.json).
- Custom roles can only be composed from existing capabilities; locked capabilities from a built-in role cannot be granted (preparation for F-10).
- docs/02-design/sitemap-and-permissions.md is the published role matrix and is linked from the R0 release notes.

=== MDV-P0-015
title: System state screens: session expired, 403 no facility access, 404, offline
type: Story
epic: MDV-P0-E02
prd: P0-05
screens: F-05
priority: MUST
roles: All
labels: ux, errors
estimate: 1
weeks: 3
depends: MDV-P0-020
confidence: 92 | Four hi-fi states with exact copy (F-05a–d).
sources: design/Medvya Access and Onboarding (F-05); docs/02-design/screen-specs.md#f-05-system-states
desc:
Implement F-05a Session expired, F-05b No access to this facility (Request access), F-05c Page not found (Back to Today), F-05d You are offline (Retry now) with the designed copy, icons and single CTA. API Problem Details 401/403/404 map to these screens; offline detection uses navigator.onLine + failed fetch.
ac:
- A 403 from any API shows F-05b with copy "You don’t have access to this facility." (microcopy key err.403) and a "Request access" action that creates an access request (stored, audited).
- An unknown route shows F-05c and states that nothing has been changed.
- Going offline shows F-05d without losing form input; reconnecting restores the page.
- Each state is keyboard-accessible, announced to screen readers, and translated (en/hi/ar) with RTL layout for Arabic.

=== MDV-P0-016
title: Data classification register and ERD → Azure SQL type mapping
type: Chore
epic: MDV-P0-E03
prd: P0-10, P0-08
covers: pkg:2
priority: MUST
roles: Developer, Founder
labels: data, compliance
estimate: 1.5
