sources: PRD v2 P0-08; docs/04-security-compliance/README.md
desc:
docs/compliance/threat-model.md (STRIDE per trust boundary: browser→BFF, BFF→SQL/Blob, jobs, future AI and integrations), data map (data categories, purposes, systems, country, processors/sub-processors), DPIA draft against DPDP Act + Rules (and a UAE health-data note), record-retention map per country (audio, transcripts, notes, audit, billing) with "to confirm by counsel" markers. Feed mitigations back as tickets.
ac:
- Threat model covers every trust boundary in docs/03-architecture/README.md with at least one mitigation per threat and links to tickets.
- Data map lists every P0–P2 data category with storage location, country and processor.
- DPIA draft lists purposes, lawful basis/consent, data minimisation, retention and risks, with open items assigned.
- Retention map has a row for each data class with proposed default and legal-review status.
- All four documents are part of the R0 delivery package.

=== MDV-P0-025
title: Secure development policies: SECURITY.md, code review rules, synthetic-only data, JIT access, offline break-glass account
type: Chore
epic: MDV-P0-E05
prd: P0-10
priority: MUST
roles: Founder, Developer
labels: security, policy
estimate: 1
weeks: 1
depends: MDV-P0-001
confidence: 90 | Content is explicit in P0-10 and the Build Plan security baseline.
sources: PRD v2 P0-10; design/Medvya Build Plan (B-05 security baseline)
desc:
SECURITY.md (reporting, supported versions), code review rules (security-sensitive code reviewed by hand, per Build Plan advice), synthetic-only test data policy (no real data in repo, CI, fixtures, AI evals), privileged access via just-in-time elevation (Entra PIM or documented manual JIT) and an offline break-glass account procedure stored outside the system.
ac:
- SECURITY.md, docs/compliance/secure-development.md and the synthetic data policy exist and are linked from README.
- PIM/JIT configuration (or documented procedure) means no standing Owner/Contributor or DB admin access in production.
- Break-glass account exists, is excluded from conditional access only as documented, credentials stored offline, and its use triggers an alert.
- A CI check rejects fixture files containing patterns of real national IDs (e.g. valid Aadhaar checksum) or obvious real data markers.

=== MDV-P0-026
title: OWASP ASVS L2 baseline: security headers, CSP, HSTS, rate limiting, input validation
type: Task
epic: MDV-P0-E03
prd: P0-10, P0-03
covers: nfr:1
priority: MUST
roles: Developer
labels: security, asvs
estimate: 1.5
weeks: 2
depends: MDV-P0-011
confidence: 88 | Explicit NFR and X1 list.
sources: PRD v2 P0 NFR 1, X1; docs/04-security-compliance/README.md#security-by-layer-software-architecture-10
desc:
Adopt OWASP ASVS L2 as the checklist (docs/compliance/asvs-l2.md with status per control). Implement strict CSP (no inline scripts), HSTS, X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy, Permissions-Policy; ASP.NET Core rate limiting on sign-in, OTP, invite acceptance and search; FluentValidation pipeline on every command; request size limits; antiforgery on state-changing BFF calls.
ac:
- Security headers score A on an automated header check against staging.
- Sign-in/OTP endpoints return 429 after the configured threshold per IP and per account.
- A command without a validator fails an architecture test.
- ASVS L2 checklist exists with each control marked Done/Planned(phase)/N/A and is reviewed in the R0 gate.

